{"name":"Bluebird Feather","hex":"#6f9db3","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bluebird","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various North American birds of the genus Sialia in the thrush family. Their plumage is blue or blue and red.","Any of various African starlings of the genus Lamprotornis, family Sturnidae, having predominantly glossy blue plumage."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Sunny and cloudless after a period of snowfall or rain.","Sunny and cloudless generally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bluebird"},{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bluebird","blocks":[{"text":"The bluebirds are a North American group of medium-sized, mostly insectivorous or omnivorous passerine birds in the genus Sialia of the thrush family (Turdidae)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Eastern_Bluebird%2C_crop.jpg/330px-Eastern_Bluebird%2C_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluebird","wikidata":"Q853286"},{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https: